Template: homelab-postfix/hostname
Type: string
Default: mail.test.mailmasker.org
Description: SMTP hostname (myhostname):
 Appears in the HELO/EHLO banner and outbound Received headers.

Template: homelab-postfix/mail_domain
Type: string
Default: test.mailmasker.org
Description: Mail domain this server is authoritative for (virtual_mailbox_domains):
 Must match the domain part of the email addresses homelab-api issues,
 or valid accounts will be rejected as unknown recipients.

Template: homelab-postfix/db_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PostgreSQL host to bootstrap the runtime role on:
 Used once, at install time, to create the narrow homelab_postfix_runtime
 role and grant it column-scoped SELECT on api.users (see
 script/homelab-postfix-bootstrap-role). Needs direct Postgres access
 (peer auth as the postgres OS user, locally or over SSH) — this is NOT
 the host Postfix connects to at runtime, see pgbouncer_host below.

Template: homelab-postfix/pgbouncer_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PgBouncer host for the runtime recipient-validation query:
 Postfix's virtual_mailbox_maps pgsql lookup connects here at runtime
 (port 6432), never straight to Postgres — see CLAUDE.md's split
 runtime/migrate role design. The bootstrapped role is also registered
 into this host's pgbouncer userlist.txt.

Template: homelab-postfix/dovecot_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: Dovecot host for LMTP delivery and SASL auth:
 Both virtual_transport (LMTP, port 24) and smtpd_sasl_path (port
 12345) point here over plain TCP — this package no longer assumes
 Dovecot is on the same host. Matches this project's own production
 mail stack, where Postfix and Dovecot run on separate hosts and talk
 over exactly these two ports (confirmed against the real production
 config, not assumed). The default of localhost preserves today's
 co-located behavior for anyone who doesn't touch this.

Template: homelab-postfix/default_process_limit
Type: string
Default: 600
Description: Postfix default_process_limit (total concurrent processes per service, e.g. smtpd):
 Postfix's own stock default is 100 -- never explicitly set by this
 package before, found live to be comfortably below what these
 containers' CPU/RAM can actually sustain. Sized to stay above
 homelab-haproxy's smtp_in/submission_in frontend maxconn (default 500
 in that package) so Postfix's own total capacity is never the first
 thing to bind for traffic HAProxy already decided to accept.

Template: homelab-postfix/client_connection_limit
Type: string
Default: 200
Description: Postfix smtpd_client_connection_count_limit (max concurrent connections from one client IP):
 Postfix's own stock default is 50. Confirmed live as the real cause of
 a stress-test mail-send collapse: HAProxy's send-proxy preserves the
 real client IP, so a burst of concurrent connections from one real
 sender hits this per-client cap long before the server's total
 capacity does. Kept as a bounded fraction of default_process_limit
 (above) rather than raised to match it 1:1 or disabled -- a single
 client still shouldn't be able to monopolize the whole process pool.

Template: homelab-postfix/behind_haproxy
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: Put this Postfix's SMTP/submission traffic behind HAProxy (homelab-haproxy)?
 Recommended, and the default: matches this project's own production
 mail stack (Postfix and Dovecot each sit behind a real HAProxy,
 confirmed live), and it's the setup that stays correct no matter where
 this host ends up -- one box today, split across two tomorrow, with
 nothing to reconfigure at the mail-transport layer either way. Set up
 homelab-haproxy in front of this host (locally, proxying to
 127.0.0.1, if this is a single-box install; on whichever host holds
 the public IP, if split) to actually receive connections once this is
 answered "true".
 .
 Answering "true" enables smtpd_upstream_proxy_protocol = haproxy,
 which then REQUIRES a PROXY-protocol preamble on every connection --
 confirmed live: until HAProxy is actually in front of this host and
 sending that preamble (send-proxy on its backend line), every direct
 SMTP connection is rejected outright with "421 Server local error"
 (the peer times out waiting for a proxy header that never arrives).
 Answer "false" only for a deliberately direct, unproxied setup (e.g.
 while still setting up homelab-haproxy) -- dpkg-reconfigure
 homelab-postfix to change this later either way, without a full
 reinstall.

Template: homelab-postfix/api_base
Type: string
Default: http://localhost:3000
Description: Base URL where homelab-api is reachable:
 Used only to register this host in homelab-api's infrastructure
 topology registry (`homelab-cli topology`) -- Postfix itself never
 calls homelab-api for anything else (its recipient/domain/alias
 lookups go straight to Postgres via pgsql: maps, not through the API).
 Best-effort: a wrong or unreachable value here only means this host
 won't show up in `homelab-cli topology`, mail flow is unaffected.

Template: homelab-postfix/advertise_host
Type: string
Default: 127.0.0.1
Description: This host's own address to advertise in the infrastructure topology registry:
 Written to homelab-api's api.infrastructure_registry via
 homelab-register-infrastructure, purely for `homelab-cli topology`
 visibility -- unlike the HTTP services' own advertise_host (which
 homelab-api's gateway actually connects to), nothing forwards traffic
 here based on this value. Leave as 127.0.0.1 only if this is a
 throwaway single-box install; set it to this host's real reachable
 address (e.g. its data-plane IP) so the topology view is actually
 useful for a distributed deployment.

Template: homelab-postfix/enable_dkim
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Wire DKIM signing into Postfix (OpenDKIM milter)?
 Adds smtpd_milters/non_smtpd_milters pointing at OpenDKIM
 (inet:localhost:8891 -- OpenDMARC is not wired here, out of scope for
 this package), and sets up /etc/opendkim/keys ownership so the
 unprivileged `homelab` user (which
 homelab-domain-admin runs as) can generate and rotate keys there. An
 already-configured host answering "false" here (the default) gets
 zero milter-config change on a routine upgrade -- turn this on
 explicitly via `dpkg-reconfigure homelab-postfix` when ready. Key
 generation/rotation itself is done separately via `homelab-cli dns
 dkim rotate/activate/retire`, not by this postinst.
